cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite the CNIG framework using the preferred citation metadata below."
title: "Constraint-Native Infrastructure Governance (CNIG): A Conceptual Framework for Component-Correct, Composition-Inadmissible Systems"

authors:
  - family-names: "Thanki"
    given-names: "Gaurav H."
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-3875-0606"

abstract: >-
  Constraint-Native Infrastructure Governance (CNIG) is a bounded conceptual
  framework for reasoning about admissible system States within Reachable State
  Space under composition constraints prior to execution. CNIG addresses
  component-correct, composition-inadmissible systems: systems in which
  individual components satisfy, or are materially consistent with, their local
  specifications, yet their composition makes a globally unintended or
  inadmissible State reachable. The framework integrates six canonical
  Primitives, four canonical Invariants, and ten canonical Failure Modes for
  analysing composition-induced Reachability, system-level Admissibility,
  Governing Constraints, Transition Paths, Effective Authority, Effective
  Capability, and Privilege Surface. CNIG is conceptual and non-operational; it
  does not execute, approve, enforce, remediate, or guarantee system changes.

